WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.690
Film Courage: What is subplot?

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:09.250
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher
and Consultant: Hmmm…what is subplot?

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:16.510
I would say a true subplot is any plot separate
from or plot line separate from the A-story

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:21.010
that has its own sort of goal and through
line right?

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:24.020
So the through line is going to be created
by the pursuit of the goal?

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:28.590
So it could be your protagonist with another
goal that they are trying to pursue I guess

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:33.340
that’s maybe the most common example that
we could pull up would be your character is

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:38.780
trying to rob a series of banks and then there
is a romantic subplot so he is also trying

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.190
to have a romantic relationship with someone
who has nothing to do with the banks right

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:48.640
it’s its own goal that has its own arc through
the movie so I would call that a true subplot.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.460
You could also have a subplot that is driven
by another character so your protagonist is

00:00:53.460 --> 00:01:00.380
robbing a series of banks unbeknownst to him
or known to him his brother is trying to win

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.220
custody of his child.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.320
So separate goal, it’s going to have it’s
own arc in the story.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.581
It probably won’t have equal weight (that’s
why we call it a subplot right) because you

00:01:09.581 --> 00:01:15.130
want your focus to be on the A-story but it
will have its own there is a separate goal

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.220
that is being pursued that has its own through
line.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.000
Film Courage: Why do we need subplot?

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.040
Naomi: I think you could argue that not all
stories, not all movies need subplots right?

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.390
Sometimes we watch movies and it just feels
like everything is connected to that A-story

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:38.380
and we don’t really have true separate subplots
but depending on the affect that you’re

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.580
trying to achieve and the sort of takeaway
message that you’re trying to give with

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.340
your story sometimes a subplot can compliment
that.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.390
I’m trying to think of a good example?

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:58.079
I feel like in Jerry Maguire the Rod Tidwell
getting his new offer from the team or whatever

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:01.979
(it’s been awhile since I’ve seen the
movie) but I feel like that was a true subplot

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:09.310
because it was (if I remember right) it was
Jerry trying to get an offer for his client

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:13.620
so it would have been Jerry’s sort of separate
goal…is that right?

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.460
Now I don’t remember.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.510
Film Courage: And I think he really needed
Rod because he was like his biggest client?

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.430
So it was kind of a stakes thing too?

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.520
Naomi: Now I am confusing myself so I’m
not sure if that’s right?

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.890
But I think that’s true so I think the subplot
was he’s trying to get a deal or an offer

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:35.150
for Rod Tidwell from Arizona his current team.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:45.209
That subplot was necessary to Jerry Maguire’s
story because it compliment his learning that

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:49.410
lesson of the theme which is that you have
to plea with heart.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:57.080
You have to stop being calculated and strategic
sometimes and just put your whole heart into

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.490
it.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:04.709
I think you can look at subplots as helping
to again teach your protagonist the lesson

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:09.590
that they need to learn from these series
of events…so Jerry gets fired and he starts

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:16.140
his own company and his romance with Dorothy
I think is a subplot because that’s a separate

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:17.140
goal.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:22.349
He is having a…they very much overlap and
sometimes they happen in the same scenes right

00:03:22.349 --> 00:03:27.860
where there are these two plot lines being
pushed forward at the same time but starting

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.280
his business and making it a success…gosh
now that I’m thinking of that the Rod Tidwell

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.300
thing might have been part of that and then
the subplot of the romance would be a separate

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.420
storyline.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.159
That would be the romantic subplot that he
is pursuing.

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:44.900
Film Courage: So can a film have many subplots?

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:45.900
Naomi: Yes.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.140
Film Courage: Oh they can, okay.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:55.500
Naomi: I definitely think so and I think that
how much weight a subplot has can vary greatly.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:04.310
Like you can have a romance subplot that is
nearly equal with the A-story or you can have

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:08.620
something that ends up looking like a runner
through your script.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:12.830
Maybe it is a minor goal that the character
is pursuing but we only need to see three

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:17.150
or four scenes where it’s like introduced
- pursued - and then accomplished (or whatever).

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:20.910
So I think you can have lots of subplots.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:29.180
My caution about having subplots would be
careful that our focus stays on the A-story

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:37.990
because if you have too many subplots and
you’re not keeping our focus on that main

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:43.090
storyline like that main conflict that we
are watching it can end up feeling like we

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:46.419
don’t know which story we’re supposed
to be paying attention to.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:50.430
It can feel sort of scattered so that’s
the only thing I would say to be careful of

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:54.509
but most people are when they come at writing
a screenplay they have a pretty good sense

00:04:54.509 --> 00:04:56.650
of what that main story that they’re trying
to tell.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:01.480
Film Courage: Well because…is it Dorothy
who is the Renée Zellweger character?

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:05.449
Because she has her own subplot in that (and
I don’t even know if you’d call it that)

00:05:05.449 --> 00:05:09.620
but that she is living with her sister and
her sister is maybe like a divorcee and older

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:16.780
and maybe a little more bitter and the great
scene of Renée and Tom coming into the women’s

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:22.490
group and they all just shoot these daggers
at Tom and he’s trying to prove that he’s

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.500
a good guy.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:29.780
And that against all odds that he is this
good guy and she has this sister character

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:33.430
who is real doubtful and just trying to fight
against that.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:38.520
So you see Renée and her situation and I
don’t know if that is also a subplot?

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:44.030
Naomi: Well so I would say that you could
look at the subplot…Renée Zellweger (Dorothy)

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:50.080
has her the romance subplot we look at it
from her point of view as well so we actually

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.020
see that relationship from both points of
view.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:58.319
I would say that her relationship with her
sister is just a relationship and it’s not

00:05:58.319 --> 00:06:04.850
its own subplot because Renée isn’t necessarily
pursuing anything, I don’t think her sister

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:05.850
is either?

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:09.990
There isn’t really a goal that we’re sort
of seeing the goal established and then seeing

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:13.050
them pursue it and conflict and then resolution
right?

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:20.169
So I would call that a relationship with a
supporting character and I would say that

00:06:20.169 --> 00:06:27.550
that supporting character is one of the benefits
of the sister is that she helps Dorothy reflect

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:34.259
on what she really wants in life, the sort
of role of love in her life, how much she

00:06:34.259 --> 00:06:37.970
needs and how much she is willing to settle
for right which is still in that same thematic.

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:39.009
Question for the viewers: Would you ever write
a movie that had zero subplots?

